    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d976">

                <pb facs="0957" n="699" xml:id="pg699"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 841.731/218</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-01-08T17:00:00+00:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 8, 1915, 5 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 7.15 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1437. The stopping of commercial cables, I am sorry to say, continues in perhaps
                    a slightly diminished number, but from forty to sixty a day are stopped out of
                    perhaps ten thousand. The chief cause given is suspicion of technical commercial
                    terms. I continue to insist on employment of men who know these terms,
                    especially in the cotton trade. But I am afraid that it will be necessary to
                    inform our commercial bodies and telegraphic companies that the exclusion of
                    every technical word from telegrams will help more than anything else.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Ambassador</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
